Here is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex regex(R"(# ([^\s]+)");
    std::regex_replace(html, regex, "<h1>$1</h1>");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Title\n\nThis is a paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace the `#` symbol followed by one or more non-space characters with an `<h1>` tag. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to perform the replacement. The regular expression `R"(# ([^\s]+)"` matches the `#` symbol followed by one or more non-space characters. The replacement string `"<h1>$1</h1>"` wraps the matched text in an `<h1>` tag. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function to convert a markdown string to HTML.

